// --- lending_account_liquidate: point the generated stub at a real liquidation scenario -------
// Measured before the fix, by calling the real instruction with the generated action's OWN
// account assembly (`#[cfg(test)] zz_probe`, this round):
//   ok=false error_code=Some(3007)
//   log| Program log: Instruction: LendingAccountLiquidate
//   log| AnchorError caused by account: asset_bank. Error Code: AccountOwnedByWrongProgram.
//        Error Number: 3007.
//   log| Left: 11111111111111111111111111111111   Right: MFv2hWf31Z9kbCa1snEPYctwafyhdvnV7FZnsebVacA
// i.e. its four `self.scout_placeholder()` accounts are `Pubkey::new_unique()` addresses nothing
// ever created, so `try_accounts` rejects the call before the handler's first line. This is
// category-1 missing glue, NOT the bracket-by-design wall `start_liquidation`/`start_flashloan`
// hit -- liquidate takes no `instructions` sysvar and has no `validate_ix_last`, and the
// hand-written `action_lending_account_liquidate_reachable` proves the very same instruction
// succeeds from one transaction once real accounts are supplied.
//
// `scout_liquidate_scenario_refresh()` (SCOUT:EXTRA-ACTIONS) rebuilds that proven scenario on
// every call and publishes it in the five `scout_liq_*` SCOUT:FIELDS; the bindings below just
// read them. The builder runs inside the FIRST binding to be evaluated (`asset_amount`, since
// the generator emits arg lets before account lets) and `return false`s the whole action on any
// failed prerequisite, so no later binding can ever read a stale/default field into a transaction.
// A builder call per binding would mint four unrelated scenarios; hence the fields.
//
// `remaining_accounts` must be a disjoint FIELD rather than a method call: the generated
// `.remaining_accounts(..)` sits inside the builder chain that already holds `&mut self.ctx`
// (identical constraint to `LendingAccountBorrow.remaining_accounts` above). The list is two
// windows back to back -- liquidatee's balances then liquidator's -- one slot per active balance
// because both banks are Fixed-priced (liquidate.rs:417-422,160-163).
//
// The two `*_accounts` args are pinned to 2 because they are LENGTHS of those windows, not free
// parameters: a fuzzed value would just mis-slice the list. `asset_amount` deliberately STAYS
// fuzzed (folded into 1..=SCOUT_LIQUIDATE_MAX_ASSET_AMOUNT) -- that is the whole reason to repair
// this action next to the fixed-amount `_reachable` sibling, since the amount is what selects
// [rationale condensed for delivery — full derivation in git history]

// --- lending_account_end_flashloan: give it an account whose health check can actually pass ----
// `end_flashloan` is NOT bracket-only, which a direct probe settled rather than assumed. Called as
// a lone one-instruction transaction against a freshly-initialized, zero-balance MarginfiAccount
// it SUCCEEDS, and succeeds again on a second call:
//   [A-end_flashloan-standalone-fresh-zero-balance] SUCCESS
//   [D-end_flashloan-standalone-fresh-second-call]  SUCCESS
// There is no `require!(account.get_flag(ACCOUNT_IN_FLASHLOAN))` on the way in -- the handler goes
// straight to `RiskEngine::check_account_init_health` (flashloan.rs:149-151).
//
// What blocked it was the account it was bound to. The shared `self.marginfi_account` carries an
// active balance from `setup()` on `self.bank`, and the health check consumes
// `get_remaining_accounts_per_bank()` remaining accounts per ACTIVE balance:
//   bound to self.marginfi_account, no remaining accounts -> 6008 InvalidBankAccount,
//     "Ran out of remaining accounts at 0" (state/marginfi_account.rs:198)
//   + remaining_accounts = [self.bank]              -> 6051 WrongNumberOfOracleAccounts,
// [rationale condensed for delivery — full derivation in git history]
