// ---- P-0033 (cumulative extraction bound) --------------------------------------------------
// marginfi's ACTUAL liquidation fee schedule, taken from the program source rather than guessed:
// `LIQUIDATION_LIQUIDATOR_FEE = I80F48!(0.025)` and `LIQUIDATION_INSURANCE_FEE = I80F48!(0.025)`
// (marginfi-v2/type-crate/src/constants.rs:24-25). liquidate.rs:216-218 turns them into the two
// discounts the whole instruction is built on:
//     liquidator_discount = 1 - f_l               (= 0.975)
//     final_discount      = 1 - (f_l + f_i)       (= 0.950)
// and then (liquidate.rs:220-241, doc comment at :57-61):
//     q_ll = q_a * p_a * liquidator_discount / p_l   -- liability the LIQUIDATOR takes on
//     q_lf = q_a * p_a * final_discount      / p_l   -- liability the LIQUIDATEE is relieved of
// So, per liquidation of gross collateral value `G = q_a * p_a`:
//     liquidator net position value change =  G - G*0.975 = +f_l         * G = +0.025 * G
//     liquidatee  net position value change = -G + G*0.950 = -(f_l+f_i)  * G = -0.050 * G
// Those two equalities are the entire property. They are exact in I80F48 -- neither leg truncates
// to an integer (only the SPL insurance transfer does, liquidate.rs:333-337, and that moves vault
// tokens, not balances) -- which is what licenses the very tight tolerance below.
const SCOUT_P33_LIQUIDATOR_FEE: fixed::types::I80F48 = fixed::types::I80F48::lit("0.025");
const SCOUT_P33_LIQUIDATOR_PLUS_INSURANCE_FEE: fixed::types::I80F48 =
    fixed::types::I80F48::lit("0.05");
// Per-ROUND slack, deliberately ~3 orders of magnitude below marginfi's own ZERO_AMOUNT_THRESHOLD
// (1e-4, constants.rs:63). The whole point of a CUMULATIVE bound is to catch a per-liquidation
// leak too small for a per-liquidation tolerance to see, so a slack as loose as the protocol's own
// dust threshold would make the accumulator worthless. 1e-6 is one native unit of the liability
// token (6 decimals in this fixture) per round. Headroom: both banks in the probe carry an
// all-zero `InterestRateConfigCompact`, so both share values stay EXACTLY I80F48::ONE and the
// share<->amount conversions are exact; the only inexactness is that the crashed asset price 0.1
// is not binary-representable, contributing < 1e-9 of error to a round's value arithmetic. So this
// slack has ~1000x headroom over the modelled error while still firing on a 1-unit-per-round grind.
const SCOUT_P33_PER_ROUND_SLACK: fixed::types::I80F48 = fixed::types::I80F48::lit("0.000001");
// Probe scenario sizing. Identical shape to `action_lending_account_liquidate_reachable` and to
// the P-0037/P-0038 probe: collateral bank at maintenance weight 0.9 priced 10 -> 0.1 by a REAL
// `lending_pool_set_fixed_oracle_price` call, liability bank priced 1.
const SCOUT_P33_LIQUIDITY_DEPOSIT_AMOUNT: u64 = 2_000_000;
const SCOUT_P33_COLLATERAL_DEPOSIT_AMOUNT: u64 = 2_000_000;
const SCOUT_P33_BORROW_AMOUNT: u64 = 1_000_000;
const SCOUT_P33_CRASHED_PRICE: f64 = 0.1;
// Optional extra deposit that leaves the LIQUIDATOR holding ASSETS in the liability bank before
// the liquidation, so `withdraw_ignore_borrow_cap` (liquidate.rs:271) takes its mixed
// asset-then-borrow path rather than the pure-borrow one. liquidate.rs:263-265 carries a standing
// `TODO: in edge cases, the liquidator starts/ends with ASSETS` on exactly that branch.
const SCOUT_P33_LIQUIDATOR_LIAB_DEPOSIT_AMOUNT: u64 = 200_000;
// 400_000 per round x 3 rounds = 1_200_000 < the liquidatee's 2_000_000 collateral, so every round
// stays on the ordinary partial path (`pre_balance >= asset_amount`, liquidate.rs:291-294) and the
// liquidatee stays maintenance-liquidatable throughout: health after round 3 is
// 0.8e6*0.9*0.1/1e6 - (1e6 - 3*38_000)*1.0/1e6 = 0.072 - 0.886 = -0.814 < 0.
const SCOUT_P33_ASSET_AMOUNT: u64 = 400_000;
const SCOUT_P33_MAX_ROUNDS: u8 = 3;
// ---- P-0021 / P-0023 (single-liquidation value bounds) ------------------------------------
//
// These two properties are the SAME measurement as P-0033 taken one round at a time, so they
// deliberately share its fee constants: `SCOUT_P33_LIQUIDATOR_FEE` and
// `SCOUT_P33_LIQUIDATOR_PLUS_INSURANCE_FEE` are marginfi's own `LIQUIDATION_LIQUIDATOR_FEE` /
// `LIQUIDATION_INSURANCE_FEE` (type-crate/src/constants.rs:24-25, both 0.025), read out of the
// program's source rather than guessed. Two sides of one accounting identity must not be able to
// disagree about the schedule they are measured against, which is why they are shared rather than
// re-declared.
//
// What is NOT shared is the tolerance. P-0033's slack scales with the round count because it bounds
// a SUM of rounds; P-0021/P-0023 each judge exactly ONE round, so the slack is one round's worth of
// I80F48 rounding. Same 1e-6 figure, different justification -- and see
// `scout_p2123_single_round_residual_is_far_below_the_slack` (SCOUT:TESTS) for the MEASURED
// residual this is set against: the observed per-round |loss - 0.05*gross| and |gain - 0.025*gross|
// are below 1e-12, i.e. the slack carries six orders of magnitude of headroom while still firing on
// a one-native-unit-per-liquidation skim (1e-6 IS one native unit of a 6-decimal token here).
const SCOUT_P2123_ROUND_SLACK: fixed::types::I80F48 = fixed::types::I80F48::lit("0.000001");
// Probe arm 2's liability-bank deposit for the LIQUIDATOR. `SCOUT_P33_LIQUIDATOR_LIAB_DEPOSIT_
// AMOUNT` (200_000) is far larger than the per-round liability the liquidator takes on
// (q_a*p_a*(1-f_l)*10^6/p_l = 400_000*0.1*0.975 = 39_000), so on that arm
// `withdraw_ignore_borrow_cap` only ever draws down assets and never borrows: the liquidator starts
// AND ends with ASSETS. 20_000 is deliberately BELOW 39_000, so round 1 draws the balance to zero
// and then borrows the 19_000 remainder -- the genuinely MIXED withdraw-then-borrow path that
// liquidate.rs:265-266 carries its standing `TODO: in edge cases, the liquidator starts/ends with
// ASSETS` on, and the one round in which the liquidator's liability-bank balance crosses sides
// inside a single transaction.
const SCOUT_P2123_LIQUIDATOR_LIAB_PARTIAL_AMOUNT: u64 = 20_000;

// ---- P-0024 (no third-party-induced liquidation) -------------------------------------------
// Arms of `action_third_party_health_probe`. EVERY arm is a transaction signed by an identity
// that is NOT the victim's authority, and no arm is the victim's own instruction. 1-4 act on the
// third party's OWN marginfi account in the SAME two banks the victim is in (the shared-state
// coupling channel); 5-6 name the VICTIM's account with the third party's signature (the literal
// authorisation channel, which `is_signer_authorized` must refuse); 7-10 are the entry points
// that take no `Signer` at all, i.e. that ANY key on earth may invoke against someone else's
// account or against a bank the victim depends on.
const SCOUT_P24_ARM_NONE: u8 = 0;
const SCOUT_P24_ARM_THIRD_PARTY_DEPOSIT_OWN: u8 = 1;
const SCOUT_P24_ARM_THIRD_PARTY_WITHDRAW_OWN: u8 = 2;
const SCOUT_P24_ARM_THIRD_PARTY_BORROW_OWN: u8 = 3;
const SCOUT_P24_ARM_THIRD_PARTY_REPAY_OWN: u8 = 4;
const SCOUT_P24_ARM_WITHDRAW_VICTIM: u8 = 5;
const SCOUT_P24_ARM_BORROW_VICTIM: u8 = 6;
// `PulseHealth` (marginfi_account/pulse_health.rs:105-109) declares exactly ONE account, a
// `mut` AccountLoader on someone else's MarginfiAccount, and no signer whatsoever.
const SCOUT_P24_ARM_PULSE_VICTIM: u8 = 7;
// `SyncIndexerFlags` (marginfi_account/sync_indexer_flags.rs:19-23): one `mut` payer Signer,
// and every account to rewrite arrives through `remaining_accounts` under `load_mut`. A funded
// stranger can therefore write to the victim's account without holding its authority. Replaces
// the `settle_emissions` arm that stood here before that instruction was removed upstream.
const SCOUT_P24_ARM_SYNC_INDEXER_VICTIM: u8 = 8;
// `LendingPoolAccrueBankInterest` (marginfi_group/accrue_bank_interest.rs:26-36): group + `mut`
// bank, no signer.
const SCOUT_P24_ARM_ACCRUE_COLLATERAL_BANK: u8 = 9;
const SCOUT_P24_ARM_ACCRUE_LIABILITY_BANK: u8 = 10;
const SCOUT_P24_ARM_COUNT: u8 = 10;

// Scenario sizing. Decimals are 6 on `bank_mint`, the collateral bank carries
// asset_weight_init 0.8 / asset_weight_maint 0.9 and RiskTier::Collateral
// (`scout_liquidation_bank_config`), the liability bank carries liability_weight 1.0. So with
// collateral 2.0 tokens and debt 15.0 tokens:
//   * at the START price 10, INITIAL health = 2*0.8*10 - 15 = +1.0, so the victim's borrow is
//     comfortably admitted by `check_account_init_health` rather than sitting on its boundary;
//   * after the admin's real `set_fixed_oracle_price` move to 8.5 (a legitimate price change,
//     applied BEFORE the pre-reading and pinned across the judged transaction), MAINTENANCE
//     health = 2*0.9*8.5 - 15 = +0.3, i.e. the victim is NOT liquidatable but is thin -- which is
//     the only interesting place to stand this property up. 8.5 and 0.9 are exact in I80F48, and
//     the 0.3 margin is ~2% of the position, three thousand times the 1e-4 tolerance, while the
//     smallest move any arm could make to the victim (arm 5 withdrawing 0.5 tokens) is
//     0.5*0.9*8.5 = 3.825, more than twelve times the margin. So the margin can neither be
//     crossed by rounding nor mask a real third-party effect. Measured: 302 probes in 15s all
//     reported pre = post = 0.299999999999976 exactly.
const SCOUT_P24_LIQUIDITY_DEPOSIT_AMOUNT: u64 = 100_000_000;
const SCOUT_P24_VICTIM_COLLATERAL_AMOUNT: u64 = 2_000_000;
const SCOUT_P24_VICTIM_BORROW_AMOUNT: u64 = 15_000_000;
const SCOUT_P24_START_PRICE: f64 = 10.0;
const SCOUT_P24_SETTLED_PRICE: f64 = 8.5;
// The third party's own position, large enough that arms 2-4 (withdraw / borrow / repay on its
// own account) all pass their own risk checks and therefore actually execute.
const SCOUT_P24_THIRD_PARTY_COLLATERAL_AMOUNT: u64 = 4_000_000;
const SCOUT_P24_THIRD_PARTY_BORROW_AMOUNT: u64 = 1_000_000;
const SCOUT_P24_PROBE_DEPOSIT_AMOUNT: u64 = 1_000_000;
const SCOUT_P24_PROBE_WITHDRAW_AMOUNT: u64 = 500_000;
const SCOUT_P24_PROBE_BORROW_AMOUNT: u64 = 1_000_000;
const SCOUT_P24_PROBE_REPAY_AMOUNT: u64 = 100_000;
// Lamports and tokens handed to the victim's OWN keypair so it can pay its own rent/fees and
// fund its own deposit. Both are ordinary fixture funding of a fresh wallet -- the same
// `create_account` / `create_token_account` builders setup() already uses for `global_fee_wallet`
// and `signer_token_account` -- and neither writes a single byte of marginfi-owned state.
const SCOUT_P24_VICTIM_LAMPORTS: u64 = 10_000_000_000;
const SCOUT_P24_VICTIM_TOKENS: u64 = 1_000_000_000_000;

// ---- P-0025 (REPAY LIVENESS) probe constants -----------------------------------------------
//
// P-0025 is a LIVENESS claim -- "a well-formed repay MUST succeed" -- so unlike every other
// property here it cannot be observed by watching committed state for something bad. It has to
// ATTEMPT the instruction. `action_repay_liveness_probe` builds ONE scenario out of real
// instructions (three banks on the shared mint, a liquidity provider, and a borrower holding
// collateral in bank C plus liabilities in banks X and Y), degrades some part of the account that
// has NOTHING to do with bank X, and then sends a partial `lending_account_repay` against X.
//
// WHAT THE SOURCE SAYS REPAY DEPENDS ON (this is what makes the property non-vacuous, and it is
// narrower than the property's own wording assumes). `lending_account_repay`
// (instructions/marginfi_account/repay.rs:34-152) constructs NO `RiskEngine` and performs NO
// health check at all -- contrast `lending_account_withdraw` (withdraw.rs:198-203) and
// `lending_account_borrow` (borrow.rs:193-198), which both call
// `RiskEngine::check_account_init_health` and therefore price EVERY active balance. Repay
// [rationale condensed for delivery — full derivation in git history]
const SCOUT_RL_ARM_COUNT: u8 = 6;
// No degradation at all: the CONTROL. Its job is to prove the probe's repay is well-formed, so a
// failure on any other arm is attributable to the degradation and not to the probe's call shape.
const SCOUT_RL_ARM_NONE: u8 = 0;
// A real `lending_pool_set_fixed_oracle_price` crash on the COLLATERAL bank (bank C), which is not
// the repaid bank. The account becomes maintenance-liquidatable; repay must still be admitted.
const SCOUT_RL_ARM_CRASH_COLLATERAL_ORACLE: u8 = 1;
// A real `lending_pool_configure_bank` pausing the OTHER liability bank (bank Y).
// `validate_bank_state(&bank, FailsInPausedState)` (repay.rs:64) reads the REPAID bank only.
const SCOUT_RL_ARM_PAUSE_OTHER_BANK: u8 = 2;
// Same instruction, `ReduceOnly` on bank Y. Repay is not even in the reduce-only set.
const SCOUT_RL_ARM_REDUCE_ONLY_OTHER_BANK: u8 = 3;
// A real oracle crash to ZERO on the other LIABILITY bank (Y): the most extreme "stale/unusable
// price on an unrelated balance" this fixture can express, and the closest thing to the
// staleness clause in the property's own wording.
