// ---- P-0026 (freshness-stamp monotonicity) --------------------------------------------------
// Subject counts. Deliberately a FIXED list of fixture-named subjects plus one probe-owned
// account rather than a dirty-tracker registry: the pure-predicate grammar admits only the
// TestContext read methods, and every account below is a plain fixture field that crucible's
// stateful pool restores in lockstep with the SVM delta it describes. No `accounts_db()` scan is
// used anywhere -- see the P-0001 block for the 957-false-positives-in-60s measurement that rules
// a scan out on this target.
const SCOUT_P26_ACCOUNT_COUNT: usize = 5;
const SCOUT_P26_BANK_COUNT: usize = 4;

// ---- P-0027 (pulse == independent re-derivation) --------------------------------------------
// Number of distinct prices the probe cycles through. Includes 0 (the boundary
// `set_fixed_oracle_price` explicitly admits, set_fixed_oracle_price.rs:41-44 rejects only
// NEGATIVE), 1, a binary-exact fraction, a sub-1e-9 dust value, and two values with more decimal
// digits than an f64 can hold -- the last two matter because the handler logs
// `price_i80.to_num::<f64>()` (set_fixed_oracle_price.rs:38), so a value that survives the store
// but not an f64 round-trip would expose any accidental f64 laundering on either side.
const SCOUT_P27_PRICE_CHOICES: u8 = 6;

// Bounded oracle drift. The generated `action_lending_pool_set_fixed_oracle_price` pins the price
// to the constant 1, so before this the only price movement in the fuzz surface came from probe
// helpers that CRASH a price (10 -> 0.1, or to 0) to manufacture a liquidatable account. Those are
// useful but unrepresentative: real oracles move in small steps, and the paths that only open on a
// modest move -- a position drifting just past the maintenance threshold, a health cache going
// marginally stale, interest and price interacting -- were never exercised.
//
// Capped at 1% per call so the movement is realistic and so a single action cannot manufacture the
// dramatic states the probes build deliberately. Repeated draws still compound, which is the point:
// a slow drift into liquidatable territory is exactly the state we could not otherwise reach.
// 5%, raised from 1%. At 1% a bank could not be walked from healthy-at-initial-margin to
// below-maintenance inside a campaign: that needs roughly a 20-30% move, i.e. ~25 consecutive
// same-direction draws of an action selected about 1 time in 140. At 5% it is ~5 draws
// (0.95^5 = 0.774, a 22.6% drop), and because the stateful pool banks each novel state the draws
// compound across iterations rather than needing to land in one sequence.
// Still deliberately bounded: a single call cannot manufacture the crash-to-zero states the
// probe helpers build on purpose, so drift stays distinguishable from a deliberate price crash.
const SCOUT_DRIFT_MAX_BPS: u64 = 500;             // 5.00%
const SCOUT_DRIFT_BPS_DENOMINATOR: u64 = 10_000;
// A floor, so drift can never take a price to zero: a zero price is its own distinct scenario that
// the probe helpers construct deliberately, and letting drift reach it would blur the two.
const SCOUT_DRIFT_MIN_PRICE: fixed::types::I80F48 = fixed::types::I80F48::lit("0.000001");

// ---- P-0028 (crank non-interference) ---------------------------------------------------------
//
// WHAT COUNTS AS A "CRANK" HERE, enumerated rather than grepped. A crank is an entry point whose
// `#[derive(Accounts)]` context declares NO `Signer<'info>` field at all, so any key on earth may
// run it against anyone's account. Parsing every `#[derive(Accounts)] pub struct X<'info>` in
// `programs/marginfi/src/instructions/**` and keeping those whose body contains zero `Signer<`
// yields exactly fourteen: kamino/harvest_reward.rs `KaminoHarvestReward`;
// marginfi_group/migrate_curve.rs `MigrateCurve`; panic_unpause_permissionless.rs
// `PanicUnpausePermissionless`; pulse_bank_price_cache.rs `LendingPoolPulseBankPriceCache`;
// accrue_bank_interest.rs `LendingPoolAccrueBankInterest`; collect_bank_fees.rs
// `LendingPoolCollectBankFees` and `LendingPoolWithdrawFeesPermissionless`;
// propagate_staked_settings.rs `PropagateStakedSettings`; propagate_fee_state.rs `PropagateFee`;
// drift/harvest_reward.rs `DriftHarvestReward`; marginfi_account/pulse_health.rs `PulseHealth`;
// emissions.rs `LendingAccountSettleEmissions` and
// `LendingAccountWithdrawEmissionsPermissionless`; liquidate_start.rs `StartLiquidation`.
//
// The six armed below are the subset that can act on THIS scenario's victim account or on one of
// the two banks its solvency depends on. `withdraw_emissions_permissionless` is deliberately NOT
// armed: the probe's banks never had emissions configured, so its `emissions_vault` PDA
// (emissions.rs:341-349) does not exist and the transaction is rejected during account
// resolution, before one line of the handler runs -- an arm that can never commit teaches
// nothing. Its ACCOUNT-side write set is byte-identical to `settle_emissions` anyway (both reach
// `claim_emissions` via `settle_emissions_and_get_transfer_amount`,
// state/marginfi_account.rs:1404-1419), so arms 2-3 already cover that surface.
const SCOUT_P28_ARM_NONE: u8 = 0;
const SCOUT_P28_ARM_PULSE_HEALTH: u8 = 1;
// `SyncIndexerFlags` (marginfi_account/sync_indexer_flags.rs:19-23) declares ONE account, a
// `mut` payer Signer, and takes every MarginfiAccount to rewrite through `remaining_accounts`,
// each loaded `load_mut`. So any funded stranger may write to any account in the program: the
// sharpest non-interference arm available, and the successor to the `settle_emissions` arm that
// stood here before that instruction was removed upstream.
const SCOUT_P28_ARM_SYNC_INDEXER: u8 = 2;
// `LendingPoolPulseBankPriceCache` again, aimed at the LIABILITY bank -- arm 6 aims the same
// crank at the collateral bank, and the pair is what distinguishes "wrote its own target" from
// "wrote the other bank too".
const SCOUT_P28_ARM_PULSE_CACHE_LIABILITY: u8 = 3;
const SCOUT_P28_ARM_ACCRUE_COLLATERAL: u8 = 4;
const SCOUT_P28_ARM_ACCRUE_LIABILITY: u8 = 5;
const SCOUT_P28_ARM_PULSE_PRICE_CACHE: u8 = 6;
const SCOUT_P28_ARM_COUNT: u8 = 6;

// A small, deliberate clock advance taken BEFORE the pre-reading, so it is part of the scenario
// and never part of what the property blames on the crank. Without it `accrue_interest`'s
// `time_delta == 0` early return (state/bank.rs:456-459) makes arms 4-5 no-ops and
// `claim_emissions`' period is zero, i.e. the cranks would be measured doing nothing. Five
// seconds is chosen to stay strictly below the `oracle_max_age: 10` every bank in this fixture is
// built with (`scout_valid_bank_config`), so no unrelated action can start failing on staleness
// because this probe ran. The mechanism (a direct `set_sysvar` on the Clock) and the slot step are
// copied from the pre-existing `action_advance_panic_pause_expiry`, which already advances this
// fixture's clock by 30 minutes, so a moving clock is not a new hazard here.
const SCOUT_P28_WARP_SECONDS: i64 = 5;
const SCOUT_P28_WARP_SLOTS: u64 = 12;
// The unrelated follow-up transaction (see the P-0028 block's LEG B). Deliberately a deposit into
// the COLLATERAL bank by the third-party account, because `scout_p24_build_scenario` performed
// exactly that deposit successfully a few instructions earlier in the same probe call
// (`scout_liquidate_deposit(third_party_account, collateral_bank, ..)`), which is what supplies
